From 198cf467cbb455025cae497d7f4219c7680167bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 02:09:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Document the last 5 undocumented module directories

Closes the remaining repo-hygiene item from July 22: only governance/,
cassettes/, and regulatory_cassettes/ had READMEs; Domain/, Engines/,
Model/, Sim/, observe/ did not.

All five turned out to be used only by the standalone simulator
(iceberg_complete_simulator.py) and its tests -- confirmed by grepping
for actual importers before writing anything, not assumed. The
production governance path (production_harness.py, sentinel_worker.py,
api_server*.py) does not import from any of them; each README says so
up front rather than leaving the reader to guess.

Content is derived directly from reading the code, not from the
existing in-file docstrings, which lean heavily on unverified
'Best-in-Class'/'deterministic'/'governance-safe' marketing language.
Where a docstring's claim didn't hold up against the actual
implementation, the README says so rather than repeating it:

- Engines/README.md: rl_ppo_adaptive.py's PPORouter doesn't implement
  PPO despite the name and docstring -- no clipped surrogate objective,
  no advantage estimation, no training loop at all. It's a fixed,
  untrained random policy with a deterministic wait-time adjustment.
  simple_rl_trainer.py is the one that actually trains (REINFORCE-style
  with a value baseline).
- Sim/README.md: cluster_runner.py's run_batch() docstring claims
  deterministic ordering via stable future completion -- collects via
  concurrent.futures.as_completed(), which yields in actual completion
  order, not a guaranteed-stable one. Noted as a real correctness
  caveat for anyone relying on output order, with the actual fix (sort
  by caller_id after collection) if that matters.
- Model/README.md: surfaces Build_Graph.py's own in-file review notes
  (mutable GraphNode.neighbors, no topology versioning, no cycle
  detection) rather than re-deriving or silently dropping them.

Also added a pointer to these five directories in the top-level
README.md's code-navigation section, which listed governance/ etc. but
had no mention of the simulator's supporting modules at all.

No code touched -- documentation only. Tests/test_graph_integrity.py
and Tests/test_system_readiness.py (the two files most directly
exercising this code) still pass, confirming nothing about the actual
behavior was changed by writing these.

Base: ledger-boot-and-ci-gates (77b7589), itself unmodified.
